title: The Doctrine of Christ – Week 8
description: Jesus, promised, pre-incarnate, Creator, eternal Son, fully God, fully man, our only salvation: the promise, the theophanies, the Creator, the eternal Sonship, the deity, the seven "I am" statements, the humanity, salvation and the names of Christ.
output: lessons/doctrine/doctrine-week-8-doctrine-of-christ.html

# Doctrine Week 8. One deck from two old pages: the main page (nine sections plus the "Before Abraham was, I am"
# feature band) and the one-page infographic ("A Visual Guide"), folded in as a closing summary slide.

=== Introduction
--- The Doctrine of Christ [title]
overline: Faith Baptist Church of Plymouth County · Adult Sunday School · Week 8
heading: The Doctrine of Christ
sub: Jesus — promised, pre-incarnate, Creator, eternal Son, fully God, fully man, our only salvation
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the study. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- Opening Scripture
big: 1 Timothy 3:16

=== I. The Promise of Christ
--- The Promise of Christ
lead: From the Garden onward, Scripture points forward to one coming Deliverer.
fig: The first prophecy of Christ | Genesis 3:15 | The first prophecy of Christ, as a coming deliverer.
fig: The promise to Abraham | Genesis 12:1-4 | The promise to Abraham, through whom the Messiah would come.

--- The Promise of Christ, continued
fig: The virgin birth foretold | Isaiah 7:14 | A prophecy of the virgin birth of Christ.
fig: Bethlehem & His eternal nature | Micah 5:2 | A prophecy of Christ’s birthplace and eternal nature.

=== II. Theophanies / Christophanies
--- Theophanies / Christophanies
lead: Old Testament appearances of Jesus — the “Angel of the {Lord}” and other pre-incarnate visitations widely understood as the Son of God, seen before Bethlehem.
fig: The Angel of the {Lord} and Hagar | Genesis 16:7-13 | The “Angel of the {Lord}” is often seen as a pre-incarnate appearance of Jesus, here appearing to the bondwoman of Abraham and Sarah.

--- The LORD at Mamre
fig: The {Lord} at Mamre | Genesis 18:1-2 | The {Lord} appearing to Abraham as a man.
fig: Captain of the {Lord}’s host | Joshua 5:13-15 | Widely understood as a pre-incarnate appearance of Jesus.

--- Melchizedek and the fourth man
fig: Melchizedek, King of Salem | Genesis 14:18-20; Hebrews 7:1-3 | The appearance of Melchizedek is seen as a type of Christ.
fig: The fourth man in the fire | Daniel 3:24-25 | A powerful visual appearance of Jesus to protect His followers.

=== III. Jesus as Creator of All Things
--- Jesus as Creator of All Things
lead: All creation, visible and invisible, was made through Him — and He sustains it still.
fig: All things made by Him | John 1:3
fig: By Him all things consist | Colossians 1:16-17 | Jesus is the source and sustainer of all creation.

--- Heir of all things
fig: Heir of all things | Hebrews 1:2
fig: He spake, and it was done | Psalm 33:6-9

=== IV. Eternal Sonship
--- Eternal Sonship
lead: Jesus as the eternal Word, co-existent with the Father before time began.
fig: The Word was God | John 1:1-2 | Jesus as the eternal Word, co-existent with the Father.
fig: Thy throne, O God, is for ever | Hebrews 1:8 | The Son is called “God,” and His throne is eternal.
fig: What is his Son’s name? | Proverbs 30:4 | A foreshadowing of the Father and the Son in the Old Testament.

=== V. The Deity of Jesus
--- The Deity of Jesus
lead: The complete essence of God dwells in Christ — He is not merely God-like, but God.
fig: The Word was God | John 1:1 | Explicitly states Jesus’ divine nature.
fig: All the fulness of the Godhead | Colossians 2:9 | The complete essence of God dwells in Jesus.
fig: The great God and our Saviour | Titus 2:13 | Calls Jesus “the great God and our Saviour.”

=== VI. The Seven “I Am” Statements
--- The Seven “I Am” Statements
lead: In the Gospel of John, Jesus repeatedly says “I am” — not just self-description, but a direct claim to deity, echoing God’s revelation to Moses as “I AM THAT I AM” ([[Exodus 3:14]]). These statements angered the Pharisees, who understood exactly what He was claiming.
group: I am the bread of life. | | John 6:35; John 6:48
group: I am the light of the world. | | John 8:12; John 9:5
group: I am the door of the sheep. | | John 10:7; John 10:9
group: I am the good shepherd. | | John 10:11; John 10:14
group: I am the resurrection and the life. | | John 11:25
group: I am the way, the truth, and the life. | | John 14:6
group: I am the true vine. | | John 15:1; John 15:5

--- Before Abraham was, I am
kicker: The most direct claim of all
p: Unlike the seven statements above, which all have a predicate — “bread,” “light,” “door” — this is an absolute declaration. The Greek behind it, *ἐγώ εἰμι* (egō eimi), is the same phrase the Greek Old Testament uses for God’s name in passages such as [[Isaiah 43:10]]. The Pharisees understood immediately that Jesus was claiming to be God — and picked up stones to kill Him for blasphemy.
big: John 8:58

=== VII. The Humanity of Jesus
--- The Humanity of Jesus
lead: Fully God, Christ also took on real flesh and blood — tempted as we are, yet without sin.
fig: Made in the likeness of men | Philippians 2:7 | Christ’s voluntary self-emptying to become a man.
fig: Should taste death for every man | Hebrews 2:9
fig: Took part of the same | Hebrews 2:14 | Jesus shared in humanity to conquer death.

--- The Humanity of Jesus, continued
fig: Made like unto His brethren | Hebrews 2:16-18
fig: Tempted like as we are, yet without sin | Hebrews 4:15
fig: The man Christ Jesus | 1 Timothy 2:5 | Jesus is the sole mediator, a man, for humanity.

=== VIII. Salvation in Jesus
--- Salvation in Jesus
lead: There is no other name under Heaven given among men whereby we must be saved.
fig: None other name | Acts 4:12 | Jesus is the only way to salvation.
fig: No man cometh unto the Father, but by me | John 14:6 | Jesus is the exclusive path to God the Father.
fig: Confess and believe | Romans 10:9

